findings.evidence:
README:91-94 says 'nothing may be added ... Any golden change, re-locked or not, fails there until a maintainer ... added the golden-reviewed label'. golden.yml:4 ('nothing added') and the first Decision bullet of ADR-0009 say the same. In the code, compare_base (check_golden.py:408) only counts res['files'] as added. Revision.scan skips __pycache__ contents (line 316, except unchecked-hash .pyc) and empty or whitespace-only __init__.py files (line 321).

I ran my own simulation of golden.yml: a GitHub-like origin with refs/pull/1/merge, a full base clone, `python -I -S base/governance/tools/merge_ref.py --fetch file://... --pr 1`, then `check_golden.py --root pr --rev HEAD --base-manifest base/tests/golden/MANIFEST.sha256`, without the label. Each of these exited with rc 0 and printed 'golden lock OK':
- deleting tests/golden/__init__.py;
- adding an empty tests/golden/numpy/__init__.py;
- adding a whitespace-only tests/golden/sub/__init__.py;
- adding tests/golden/__pycache__/conftest.py;
- adding a timestamp-based .pyc in that __pycache__.

As a control, gut plus relock gave rc 1 without the label and rc 0 with it, and twins, 8.3 names and unchecked .pyc gave rc 1 with or without the label.

None of these makes a false pass possible. With the init removed, `check_golden.py --run` still passed 21 of 21. Adding the empty numpy package as well made the isolated run fail with '1 errors' (it shadows numpy), so the failure is closed. CODEOWNERS on /tests/golden/ still applies. So this is a documentation inaccuracy in the task's 'make the README accurate' deliverable, not a hole in the gate.
